          "distinct": true,
          "id": "3bbc9bde2533869773fcbc683cf5ea7853db8c42",
          "message": "Speed up f64 lgamma reflection by folding the recurrence log into the sine\n\nThe `z < ½` reflection computes `ln π − ln|sin πz| − ln Γ(1−z)`.  When `1−z`\nlands in the central-table band, `ln Γ(1−z)` is a table value `cell_eval(d)`\nplus the recurrence's `ln ∏` — so the fast leg took *two* logarithms there\n(`ln|sin πz|` and `ln ∏`) where CORE-MATH's reflection takes one.\n\nFold the recurrence product into the sine before the logarithm: a single\n`ln(|sin πz|·∏^±1)` now replaces both logs.  Combining is at least as accurate\nas the two separate logs (one transcendental rounding instead of two, and\n`|ln(ab)| ≤ |ln a|+|ln b|`), so the existing `LGAMMA_TABLE_ERR` gate still\ncertifies the leg — `tests/lgamma.rs`'s worst-case corpus passes bit-for-bit.\n\nlgamma_reflect (`[-10,-2]` band): 65.1 → 55.6 ns, 2.16× → 1.84× vs CORE-MATH.\nThe Stirling sub-band (`1−z ≥ 8`) keeps the two-log form (its `ln w` is scaled\nby `w−½`, not addable to the sine) — at structural parity with CORE-MATH there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-17T22:15:43+08:00",
          "tree_id": "1fd473142912cc6515e4ea84030fc4e9d6c5abb0",
