          "distinct": true,
          "id": "b5152540fe1f1406675cf7a2250691551fc77636",
          "message": "Add correctly rounded binary128 atan2q\n\nReduce on dyadic breakpoints: one float divide picks i = round(64·min/max)\nwithin 0.505, and since i/64 is a 6-bit dyadic both sides of\ntan(θ − atan(i/64)) are exact 128-bit integers (dn ≤ 7 whenever i ≥ 1).  A\nfloat-seeded Newton reciprocal — no integer division anywhere — yields the\nreduced tangent to 128 bits on the fast leg and 384 on the accurate one,\nalways from below so every residual stays nonnegative.  The atan(i/64) table\nand the quadrant offsets add back in the same top-limbs-shared 256/384 frame\nsplit as logq; the first-quadrant i = 0 band keeps atan(t) floating all the\nway down through subnormal results to underflow-to-zero, which only the\naccurate leg's rounder handles — the fast leg rounds on a fixed 15-bit guard\nand bails below f128::MIN_EXP.\n\nThe polynomial runs latency-first: the even and odd halves of the Taylor sum\nare independent Horner chains in T⁴ with 64-bit tails, merged after T³ has\nalready multiplied in.  Quadrant signs select by mask, not branch.\n\nConstants come from tools/gen_atan2_f128.py — atan(i/64), the offsets, the\nTaylor coefficients 1/(2k+3), and 3π/4 for the infinity edges — which also\naudits that every exact-ratio table sum clears its nearest rounding boundary\nby 2^-121.3 or more, so the 384-bit frame decides such inputs outright.\n\nGates: bit-exact vs core_math::atan2q on CORE-MATH's 269 282-pair corpus,\na 500k-pair MPFR precision-113 sweep, and the in-source ziv_soundness\ncertification: worst fast-leg |err|/gate = 0.0720 over 200 000 quadrant-mixed\nsamples, a 13.9× margin.  Same-run CORE-MATH ratio 1.24× (75.8 ns vs\n61.3 ns median, banded ±20 draws); the ablation trail and the lead for the\nremaining gap are recorded in the perf playbook.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
          "timestamp": "2026-08-24T04:54:06+08:00",
