          "distinct": true,
          "id": "e7638e9174d6865b3d3a5d71dc0066f28f87e4c7",
          "message": "Fold the unit operand of atanq through its own reduction\n\natanq was atan2q(x, 1) — an exact ratio paying the full bivariate front:\nthe sorted split of both operands and the float divide that picks the\nsector.  Folding the unit through by hand keeps both legs, the tables,\nand the certified Ziv gate while the reduction collapses: i = round(64·x)\nbecomes an integer shift below one and a single hardware divide on the\ntop 48+dn bits above it (within 0.503 of 64·t), one side of every exact\nproduct becomes a shift, and below the first breakpoint the reduced\ntangent *is* the input significand — no quotient, no Newton reciprocal,\nboth legs run the Taylor sum on exact bits.\n\nThe folded path ships its own certification exercising the entry's exact\ncode (atan_fast_leg_is_sound): worst |err|/gate = 0.0543, an 18× margin.\n\nSame-run CORE-MATH ratio, bench atanq at x86-64-v3: 1.26× → 1.02×\n(85.7/67.9 ns → 52.8/51.7 ns on a quiet box).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
          "timestamp": "2026-08-31T03:48:44+08:00",
          "tree_id": "c70da99e23e4f5ae07eea76425fb71feec983555",
