          "distinct": true,
          "id": "755fb7caf790aad8f0a28be2ce27e89ea5a7a7df",
          "message": "Add nightly-std/glibc lanes to the f128 benches and an MPFR ulp survey\n\nNightly's `f128` methods are glibc's `*f128`, not an implementation of\ntheir own, so they are a third baseline distinct from libquadmath. Nine\nbenches lacked that lane; `exp10q` gets a `glibc::exp10f128` one, since\nglibc has the symbol but std binds no `f128::exp10`.\n\n`examples/f128_ulp_survey.rs` is the accuracy half: max ulp of metallic /\nglibc / libquadmath against MPFR at 300 bits over the benches' own bands.\nCompetitor entry points resolve through `dlopen`/`dlsym` and are asserted\nwith `dladdr` — a plain `extern \"C\" fn sqrtf128` binds to\n`compiler_builtins`' own copy, which silently measures Rust's code. A\n`self_check` pins the ulp scaling against exact midpoints, and the\nmetallic column (≤ 0.5 on all 20) is the harness' own proof.\n\nFindings, now in README § Baselines: glibc and libquadmath are\nbit-identical outside `sqrt` and `hypot` (shared ldbl-128 ancestry) and\ntime within a few percent; metallic is 7-31x faster than both. glibc's\nworst is `log1pq` 2.33 ulp; libquadmath's `sqrtq`/`hypotq` are not\ncorrectly rounded (25% of draws) where glibc's are.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
          "timestamp": "2026-09-04T23:01:35+08:00",
          "tree_id": "4e7377204c78f149b22075764ea78999d886aed4",
