          "distinct": true,
          "id": "38f7ecdfabb728f462fcf74fd5d48241bda9762a",
          "message": "Stop using f128:: methods as oracles and scope the std-lane note to linux-gnu\n\n`test_sqrtq` was the one f128 sweep in the tree whose oracle was a std method\n(`f128::sqrt`) rather than `core_math::sqrtq`, resting on the x86-64-Linux fact\nthat a `sqrtf128` reference binds `compiler_builtins`' IEEE copy.  That is not\nportable: LLVM's `RuntimeLibcalls` defaults every `*_F128` math libcall to the\n*long double* name, and only the GNU-gated `X86_F128_Libcalls`/XCore and\nPowerPC system libraries override it.  On `aarch64-apple-darwin`, where\n`long double` is `double` and `sinl`/`sqrtl`/`powl` are the same symbol\naddresses as `sin`/`sqrt`/`pow` in `libsystem_m.dylib`, `f128::sqrt` lowers to\n`_sqrtl`; AAPCS64 passes the `fp128` in `q0` and the `double` callee reads\n`d0`, so it returns the f64 square root of the argument's *low 64 bits*.\nMeasured on that host, `f128::sqrt(x)` bit-equals `f64::sqrt(low64(x))`\nzero-extended on 20000/20000 draws, and `test_sqrtq`'s own sweep disagrees\nwith `metallic::sqrtq` on 1000000/1000000 points.  Point it at\n`core_math::sqrtq`, which is what every sibling `test_<fn>q` already uses and\nwhat `test_sqrtq_worst_cases` uses four lines down.\n\n`fma128` rests on the same fact — `x.mul_add(y, a)` is glibc's `fmaf128` on\nlinux-gnu and `_fmal` elsewhere, where it returns the narrow function of the\nlow halves (`fma128(2, 3, 1)` is `+0.0` on Apple arm64).  Its only call site\nis `asin.rs`'s `#[cfg(all(test, feature = \"mpfr\"))] mod ziv_soundness`, so\nnothing ships broken and no release object references `_fmal`; say so in the\ndoc comment rather than leave \"correctly rounded\" unqualified.\n\nCLAUDE.md asserted the std lanes are glibc without qualification.  Scope it,\nand record the rest of the failure surface: the `std::sys::cmath` half (`tan`,\n`cbrt`, `hypot`, `asin`, `atan2`, `exp_m1`, `ln_1p`, the hyperbolics)\nreferences `<fn>f128` symbols Darwin does not have and fails to link, which is\nthe honest failure; the intrinsic-backed half links and lies, and a \"high half\nis zero\" check does not catch it (`f128::sin(1.0f128)` is exactly `1.0`,\nbecause `sin(±0)` returns without writing `v0`).  Fixed in LLVM main by\nllvm#214944, not yet in rustc's `rustc/23.1-2026-07-22` branch; rustc already\ncomputes `target_has_reliable_f128_math = false` here and gates only doctests\non it.\n\nVerified: `cargo test` green (17 lib + 205 integration + doctests), and\n`cargo +nightly check --features f128 --lib` clean.  The f128 *tests* cannot\nbe built on Darwin at all — `core-math-sys` fails on `vendor/src/binary128/`\nwith `__float128 is not supported on this target`, which reproduces under\n`-arch x86_64` too — so the `sqrtq.rs` change is unexercised here; it is a\none-symbol swap to the oracle already used identically in the same file and in\nevery sibling.  Needs a run on x86-64 GNU/Linux.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
